# Git Workflow and Versioning

Applies to any coding change in any workspace this rule is installed in. Procedures and examples: `.cursor/skills/git-workflow-and-versioning/SKILL.md`.

## Atomic commits (mandatory)

One logical change per commit, in every repo. Many small commits, never one large mixed commit. Commit after each verified slice without being asked; do not wait.

- After you write a hunk, **commit it before you edit it again**. One hunk → one `git apply --index` (or equivalent) → one commit.
- Rewriting a line you wrote but have **not** committed is a **second change**: commit the current tree, then edit, then commit the edit. Do not fold a write + rewrite + formatting pass into one dirty blob.
- Do not `git commit --amend` to hide a second change unless the user asked and amend is allowed.
- Do not mix refactor + feature + formatting.
- Message prefix: `feat`, `fix`, `refactor`, `test`, `docs`, `chore`.
- Before committing: review the staged diff and run relevant tests/lint.

## Branches

Tiny isolated fixes go on the active branch. Non-trivial work (multi-file, risky refactor, experiment, parallel work) gets its own branch first: `feature/` | `fix/` | `refactor/` | `chore/` + short description. Keep `main` stable and branches short-lived.

Sync with the base branch often, re-verify after resolving conflicts, and never force-push a shared branch unless asked.

## Scope

Commit only files belonging to the current task; never `.env`, secrets, build artifacts, or unrelated generated files. If unrelated local changes exist, keep them out of the commit. In summaries, state what changed and what was intentionally left untouched.
